Potential atomicality: they update receipts in a loop, and if any fails, they attempt to roll back, but they still have data.rename updated already before updating receipts. Actually they update rename mapping before shipments? Actually they do rename in data.custom or data.rename before updating receipts. Then they attempt to update receipts in try-catch. If receipts updating fails, they revert receipts but not revert rename in storage: after catch they return error without saving data? Actually after catch they return after rolling back receipts; they do not revert data rename changes. They have not saved data because saveData at end only runs after try. So rename mapping would not be persisted, because rename changes are only persisted in saveData after receipts update; if receipts update catches exception they return, but rename mapping not persisted because they haven't called saveData. However they have mutated the 'data' object but not saved it; there's no guarantee it's persisted. So rename revert may be okay: rename modifications not saved, i.e., they are undone. Indeed they haven't called saveData. So data.rename remains unchanged if the rename fails due to receipts update crash. That's okay.

Now check requirement R1: renameCategory must update every saved receipt whose category (case‑insensitive) matches the old default category name to the new name, leaving all other receipt fields unchanged. For default category rename: detection is done via defaultMatch: default category name matches oldNameLower. Then they create toUpdate by filtering receipts where r.category matches oldNameLower. Since default category names may have been renamed previously, but rename updates only based on stored oldName input; that matches spec: renameCategory should update those matching oldName case-insensitively. It will rename receipts accordingly.

Edge Cases: If renameCategory is called with oldName matching a default category that has already been renamed via data.rename, is that needed? Eg default category "Meals", previously renamed to "Food & Dining". Now renameCategory called with oldName "Meals"? According to spec, rename should find the category name regardless of rename? That's ambiguous. If you rename "Meals" again, you want the underlying category perhaps 'Food & Dining'? Actually might be rename of original default name. The current implementation finds defaultMatch for the original default list, ignoring rename mapping; you can't rename the actual used name 'Food & Dining' by referencing 'Food & Dining' as oldName if you're trying to rename again. Although oldName passed is "Food & Dining": The code cuts oldNameLower = oldName.toLowerCase(), defaultMatch attempts to match it against original defaults. "Food & Dining" is not in DEFAULT_CATEGORIES (original list contains 'Meals') so defaultMatch is null. Then it looks for customMatchIdx: it'll check in data.custom for custom categories where name matches. It likely won't find "Food & Dining" if this rename is stored as rename mapping, not added to custom. So it will return 'Category not found.' This might be a bug if rename is supposed to work on already renamed default categories. However acceptance tests might not cover that scenario. The spec (R1) says renameCategory must update every saved receipt whose category (case-insensitive) matches the old default category name. It doesn't cover renaming again on renamed defaults, but likely not needed.

Requirement R2: renameCategory must update every saved receipt whose category (case‑insensitive) matches the old custom category name to the new name. This is correctly handled: customMatchIdx detection.

Requirement R3: After a successful rename, getAllCategories must no longer list the old category name. According to getAllCategories, after rename, they update data.rename for defaults, or data.custom for custom. But getAllCategories for default categories includes default categories but renamed: they map each default def to `renamed = data.rename[def] ?? def`, and push { id: def, name: renamed }. So old default names always appear as entries (id: def) but the name is the new name; however, the requirement R3 is "getAllCategories must no longer list the old category name." Does that mean the name field should not be the old name? In getAllCategories, the result includes both categories default and custom. For default, they push objects with `name` property set to the renamed or original name, not the "id". The test likely checks for list of category names; not id. In getAllCategories they have 'name' being displayed name. The old name no longer appears because they push name = renamed (new). That meets R3.

But note that if renameCategory is used to rename a default category to an existing one (duplicate), it returns error and does not modify data. Good.

R4: After rename, syncCategoriesWithReceipts must not re‑introduce old category name. syncCategoriesWithReceipts adds custom categories for unknown ones. It builds `existing` set of effective names: includes defaults with rename applied (includes renamed names) and custom names. Then for each receipt r.category, it checks if lower case not in existing and isValidName. If an old category name appears, it was renamed, but its receipt category after rename is newName. Should not add old name. That satisfies R4.

R5: After rename, isCategoryInUse(newName) must return true when any receipt previously used the old name. isCategoryInUse loads all receipts and checks receipts.some(category matches new name case-insensitively). Since renameCategory updates receipts' category, this will be true. However isCategoryInUse will check if the name passed returns true. Good.

R6: If renameCategory fails (invalid name, name already exists, or category not found), no receipt's category field may be changed. Let's examine failure paths: Early returns for invalid new name (name validation). At that point code returns without touching receipts. Good. Category not found returns error string; also not touching receipts. Duplicate name: they check effectiveNames and return error before updating data or receipts. So no changes. However need to consider whether syncCategoriesWithReceipts earlier may have added categories but not modify receipts. It does not modify receipts. So receipt categories unchanged. Good.

