We need to examine other aspects: renameCategory implementation, validateMileageMiles behavior, UI; other UI requirements probably not directly testable.

We should inspect src/lib/categories.ts to see if it fulfills all renameCategory requirements: updates receipts category for default and custom categories, case-insensitively; after rename, old name should not be in getAllCategories; syncCategoriesWithReceipts must not re-introduce old name; isCategoryInUse(newName) true after rename when receipts used old name; renameCategory fails must keep receipts unchanged.

Let's examine renameCategory code carefully.

- renameCategory(store, oldName, newName):
  - lastUsedStore set
  - Validates newName length & characters
  - Calls syncCategoriesWithReceipts(store) before loading data; ensures categories reflect receipts.
  - loadData gets data.
  - Determine defaultMatch: find default category name that matches lowercased oldName; customMatchIdx: find index in data.custom matching lowercased oldName.

  - If both undefined, return 'Category not found.'

  - Build effectiveNames set: includes rename[def] (or def) lowercased for each default, and custom category names lowercased.

  - newNameLower = newName.toLowerCase()
  - They check if effectiveNames.has(newNameLower) and not (special case). This is to prevent duplicate, but allow rename to same name? Possibly aside.

Check duplicate detection: if effectiveNames.has(newNameLower) && not (some condition) => duplicate. That condition tries to allow same name when renaming to existing mapping? Let's parse:

```
if (effectiveNames.has(newNameLower) && !(
    (defaultMatch && (data.rename[defaultMatch]?.toLowerCase() === newNameLower)) ||
    (customMatchIdx !== -1 && data.custom[customMatchIdx].toLowerCase() === newNameLower)
  )) {
    return 'Category already exists.';
}
```

So, if new name already appears in effectiveNames, but it's allowed if it's the same as current mapping (for default: if rename exists and maps to that same new name? Actually condition: defaultMatch && (data.rename[defaultMatch]?.toLowerCase() === newNameLower). If defaultMatch is the old default, data.rename[defaultMatch] might be undefined or already set. If it's undefined, then undefined?.toLowerCase() is undefined, so not equal; but effectiveNames includes rename[defaultMatch] or default name. So if renaming default "Meals" to "Meals" (same case), effectiveNames contains "Meals". The condition wouldn't allow because rename[def]? undefined; defaultMatch && rename[defaultMatch]?.toLowerCase() === newNameLower? false. customMatchIdx check same: data.custom[customMatchIdx].toLowerCase() === newNameLower would be true if custom name unchanged (renaming to same). So allow rename to same (no change). That's ok.

Now they prepare newData: copy custom, hidden, rename.

If defaultMatch, set newData.rename[defaultMatch] = newName. Good.

Else custom => custom old name replaced with newName for that index.

If there was a rename entry for old custom name, they also preserve rename mapping: newData.rename[newName] = newData.rename[oldCustom]; delete newData.rename[oldCustom]; This ensures rename for custom? Not sure.

Now they update receipts:

```
const receipts = await getAllReceipts(store);
const toUpdate = receipts.filter((r) => r.category && typeof r.category === 'string' && r.category.toLowerCase() === oldNameLower);
const originals = toUpdate.map((r) => ({ ...r }));
try {
    for (const r of toUpdate) {
      const updated = { ...r, category: newName } as any;
      await store.put('receipts', updated);
    }
    // All receipt updates succeeded; now save category changes.
    await saveData(store, newData);
    return null;
  } catch (e) {
    // Roll back receipt changes.
    for (const orig of originals) await store.put('receipts', orig as any);
    return 'Failed to rename receipts';
  }
```

Assume store's put method writes into receipts collection; but we need to verify store.put signature: Look at storage file.

